1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,561
We are now inside the Hall
of Organ Pipes, one of the
most spectacular settings
on the entire route through
the Grotta del Bue Marino.
This is where the most
imposing and impressive

2
00:00:12,561 --> 00:00:24,705
formations are concentrated.
The hall is characterised
mainly by four large
columns, among the oldest
formations visible on the
tourist route: their age
is estimated at around

3
00:00:24,705 --> 00:00:36,294
500,000 years. But how
do these structures form?
It all begins at the
surface, with rain.
Rainwater, as it seeps into
the mountain, passes through
the soil and becomes

4
00:00:36,294 --> 00:00:48,369
enriched with carbon
dioxide. When water and
carbon dioxide combine,
a slightly acidic solution
forms, capable of eroding
the limestone. As the water
works its way inside the

5
00:00:48,369 --> 00:00:59,542
mountain, it dissolves the
calcium carbonate, the
constituent element of the
rock, transforming it into
calcium bicarbonate,
a soluble form that
is carried along

6
00:00:59,542 --> 00:01:11,825
in solution. When this
mineral-rich water reaches
the underground cavities,
such as this hall, the
process changes. If a drop
remains suspended from the
ceiling, it loses carbon

7
00:01:11,825 --> 00:01:24,455
dioxide and slowly deposits
calcium carbonate, which
crystallises and solidifies
to form a stalactite.
If instead the drop falls
to the ground, the mineral
is deposited on the floor,

8
00:01:24,455 --> 00:01:35,905
giving rise to a stalagmite.
When, over the course
of thousands of years,
a stalactite and
a stalagmite meet and join
together, they form
a column, like those we see

9
00:01:35,905 --> 00:01:47,772
here. These are extremely
slow processes. It can take
approximately 100 years
to form just one cubic
centimetre of formation.
This means that every
structure we see is the

10
00:01:47,772 --> 00:02:00,333
result of immensely long
geological timeframes and
a constant balance between
water, rock, and atmosphere.
In this hall, we can read,
almost like in a natural
archive, the invisible

11
00:02:00,333 --> 00:02:12,200
journey of water through the
heart of the mountain. Every
formation is the concrete
trace of that silent
journey, repeated drop
by drop for hundreds
of thousands of years.
